      "message": "So, Could use this -\u003e\n```\n- (id)stringByExpandingTildeInPath {\n    let path \u003d to_rust_string(env, this);\n    log!(\"Path is {:?}\", path.to_string());\n\n    let new_path_str \u003d if path.starts_with(\u0027~\u0027) {\n        let path \u003d path.trim_start_matches(\u0027~\u0027);\n        assert!(!path.contains(\u0027~\u0027));\n        let guest_path \u003d env.fs.home_directory().join(path);\n        log!(\" The Guest Path {:?}\", guest_path);\n        let resolved \u003d fs::resolve_path(\u0026guest_path, None);\n        log!(\" Resolved is {:?}\", resolved);\n        format!(\"/{}\", resolved.join(\"/\"))\n    } else {\n        path.to_string()\n    };\n\n    log_dbg!(\"[(NSString *){:?} stringByExpandingTildeInPath] {} -\u003e {}\", this, path, new_path_str);\n\n    let new_string \u003d from_rust_string(env, new_path_str);\n    autorelease(env, new_string)\n}\n```\n\nFor Dead or Alive BlackJack the if statement falls through as the passed path is \"/var/mobile/Applications/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/Documents/app.sp\" (no tilde)\n\n\n For Galaxy on Fire the path passed into the function is something like \"~/Library/GOF\" which initially joins as \"/var/mobile/Applications/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000//Library/GOF\" but the resolved and then format join corrects the double //\n \nI did not test for anything other than those. Doing that now...",
